// @test: run
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: bounds, protocols, callables
// @timeout: 20000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @description: A callable satisfies an `Fn` bound — a plain function and
//   a closure alike.

// There is no `type Fn is protocol` anywhere in core/ and no
// `implement … Fn … for` either, so `implements` — which answers from the
// impl registry — could never say yes, and all 71 `: Fn(…)` / `: FnOnce(…)`
// bounds across 11 stdlib files were unsatisfiable. A plain function and a
// closure failed identically:
//
//     error<E405>: type `fn(Int) -> Int` does not implement `Fn`,
//                  required by this call's bound
//
// The compiler already HELD the fact and used it in one direction only:
// `specialization_selection.rs` answers "functions implement Fn traits"
// when deciding a NEGATIVE bound. The positive side was started and left
// as an empty branch — `infer/decls.rs` carries the
// `matches!(proto_name, "Fn" | "FnOnce" | "FnMut")` arm with a comment
// where the body should be.
//
// Fn is now answered structurally, like Deref: from the type's own shape,
// not from a registry entry nothing writes. The companion spec
// `fn_bound_refuses_a_non_callable.vr` is the half that keeps this from
// being a blanket yes — 71 unsatisfiable bounds must not become 71
// unchecked ones (T1066).

mount core.prelude.*;

pure fn square(x: Int) -> Int { x * x }

public fn apply<F>(f: F, x: Int) -> Int
where F: Fn(Int) -> Int
{
    f(x)
}

fn main() -> Int {
    // A named function.
    assert_eq(apply(square, 3), 9);
    // A closure, through the same bound.
    assert_eq(apply(|y: Int| y + 1, 41), 42);
    print("fn-bound: callables accepted");
    0
}
